2025-11-27 06:36:25,733 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concise, data-backed orientation to AI that you can cite in a class project. I’ve organized it into: core concepts, why AI is surging now, measurable capabilities, adoption and investment, economic and labor impacts, risks and externalities, governance and standards, and directions for further exploration. Each section includes specific numbers and primary sources.

1) What AI is (and the main technical ideas)
- Artificial intelligence: systems that perform tasks typically requiring human intelligence (perception, reasoning, language, decision-making). Modern AI is largely machine learning (ML) that learns patterns from data, with deep learning using multi-layer neural networks.
- Key model family: transformers (2017–). They power large language models (LLMs) and multimodal models, enabling generative AI (text, images, code).
- Foundation models: large pre-trained models adapted to many downstream tasks. The Stanford AI Index documents that since 2014, most state-of-the-art models are built by industry and pre-trained on massive datasets and compute.
Sources:
- Stanford AI Index Report 2024: https://aiindex.stanford.edu/report/
- “Attention Is All You Need” (Vaswani et al., 2017): https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762

2) Why AI is surging now (scale of data, models, and compute)
- Compute scaling: Training compute for notable AI systems grew extraordinarily fast from 2012–2018 (doubling every ~3.4 months), and remains a primary driver of capability growth.
Source: OpenAI “AI and Compute” analysis: https://openai.com/index/ai-and-compute/
- Model scale: GPT-3 (2020) had 175B parameters; PaLM (2022) had 540B. Model counts alone don’t determine capability, but illustrate scaling trends.
Sources:
  - Brown et al. (2020), GPT-3: https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165
  - Chowdhery et al. (2022), PaLM: https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.02311
- Compute and data center demand: Data centers consumed ~460 TWh (2022) and are projected to reach 620–1,050 TWh by 2026, driven in part by AI workloads.
Source: IEA Electricity 2024: https://www.iea.org/reports/electricity-2024 and commentary https://www.iea.org/commentaries/data-centres-and-ais-growing-electricity-demand

3) Measurable capabilities (benchmarks and scientific milestones)
- General exams and benchmarks: OpenAI’s GPT-4 Technical Report reports performance at or near top human percentiles on several standardized exams (e.g., ~90th percentile on the Uniform Bar Exam after practice effects accounted for). While some tests can be susceptible to contamination, this illustrates rapid benchmark gains.
Source: OpenAI GPT-4 Technical Report (2023): https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08774
- Protein structure prediction: AlphaFold expanded its database to over 200 million predicted protein structures in 2022—covering nearly all cataloged proteins—dramatically accelerating research in biology.
Sources:
  - DeepMind announcement: https://www.deepmind.com/blog/alphafold-reveals-the-structure-of-the-protein-universe
  - EMBL-EBI AlphaFold DB: https://alphafold.ebi.ac.uk/

4) Adoption and investment
- Organizational use: 65% of respondents say their organizations are regularly using generative AI in 2024, up from 33% in 2023; 55% have adopted it in at least one business function.
Source: McKinsey, The State of AI in 2024: https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/quantumblack/our-insights/the-state-of-ai-in-2024
- Private investment: Global private AI investment was approximately $67.2 billion in 2023 (down from the 2021 peak but still historically high). The U.S. continues to attract the largest share.
Source: Stanford AI Index 2024 (Private Investment chapter): https://aiindex.stanford.edu/report/

5) Economic impact and measured productivity effects
- Macro potential: Generative AI could raise global GDP by ~7% and lift productivity growth by ~1.5 percentage points over 10 years, contingent on adoption and complementarity with labor and capital.
Source: Goldman Sachs Global Economics Research (2023): https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/pages/gs-research/gen-ai-could-raise-global-gdp-by-7-percent.html
- Field experiments and RCTs:
  - Customer support at a Fortune 500 firm: generative AI assistance increased agent productivity by 14% on average, with the largest gains for less-experienced workers.
    Source: Brynjolfsson, Li, Raymond (2023), NBER Working Paper No. 31161: https://www.nber.org/papers/w31161
  - Professional writing tasks: participants using GPT-4-based tools completed tasks 37% faster and produced higher-quality outputs (expert-blind ratings).
    Source: Noy & Zhang (2023), Experimental Evidence on the Productivity Effects of Generative AI: https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4375283
  - Software development: a controlled GitHub study found developers completed coding tasks 55% faster with GitHub Copilot for specific tasks.
    Source: GitHub research blog (2022): https://github.blog/2022-09-07-research-quantifying-github-copilots-impact-on-developer-productivity/

6) Risks and externalities (with data where available)
- Misinformation and safety incidents: Public incident databases document model failures and harms (e.g., biased outputs, unsafe recommendations). While counts vary, incident tracking is growing as models are deployed.
Source: AI Incident Database (Responsible AI Collaborative): https://incidentdatabase.ai/
- Environmental footprint:
  - Sector-level: See IEA figures above (620–1,050 TWh by 2026 for data centers).
  - Training emissions vary widely by hardware, data center efficiency, and energy mix. A 2019 analysis estimated training a large NLP model with then-common practices could emit ~284 tons CO2e; later work shows orders-of-magnitude reductions possible with efficient hardware and low-carbon power.
    Sources:
    - Strubell et al. (2019): https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.02243
    - Patterson et al. (2021), Google: https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.10350
- Labor market reallocation:
  - Automation exposure is uneven across occupations. A 2023 macro analysis estimated tasks equivalent to ~300 million FTEs could be affected globally (substitution plus augmentation); net outcomes depend on policy and investment.
    Source: Goldman Sachs (2023): link above

7) Governance, policy, and standards (what’s in force or imminent)
- EU AI Act (adopted 2024): A risk-based framework with prohibited uses (e.g., certain biometric categorization), high-risk obligations (conformity assessments, quality management, data governance, transparency), and lighter duties for limited-risk systems. Most provisions phase in over 2–3 years after entry into force; prohibitions apply earlier.
Source: Council of the EU press release (May 21, 2024): https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2024/05/21/council-adopts-ai-act/
- United States:
  - Executive Order 14110 (Oct 30, 2023): Directs standards, safety testing (including red-teaming), reporting for large training runs, and guidance on critical infrastructure, national security, privacy, and civil rights.
    Source: https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/presidential-actions/2023/10/30/executive-order-on-safe-secure-and-trustworthy-artificial-intelligence/
  - NIST AI Risk Management Framework (RMF 1.0, Jan 2023): Voluntary guidance for identifying, assessing, and managing AI risks across the lifecycle.
    Source: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework
- International standards:
  - ISO/IEC 42001:2023: First AI management system standard (AIMS) providing requirements for governing AI across organizations (risk, monitoring, human oversight).
    Source: https://www.iso.org/standard/81230.html
- China:
  - Interim Measures for the Management of Generative AI Services (effective 2023): Provider responsibilities for security assessments, data governance, labeling, and content moderation.
    Sources:
    - Original (Chinese): https://www.cac.gov.cn/2023-07/13/c_1690898327029107.htm
    - Stanford DigiChina English translation/analysis: https://digichina.stanford.edu/work/translation-chinas-finalized-rules-on-generative-ai/
- Multilateral coordination:
  - Bletchley Declaration (Nov 2023): 28 countries and the EU committed to cooperate on AI safety research and evaluation (esp. frontier systems).
    Source: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-safety-summit-2023-bletchley-declaration

8) What to understand for a strong project (key takeaways with pointers)
- Capabilities are scaling with compute and data: See OpenAI compute trends; GPT-4, AlphaFold milestones.
- Adoption is broad and accelerating in enterprises: 65% regular genAI use in 2024 (McKinsey).
- Early, credible productivity evidence exists in specific workflows: 14% in call centers (NBER), 37% in writing RCT, 55% faster on coding tasks (GitHub).
- Investment remains high despite market cycles: $67.2B in private AI investment in 2023 (AI Index).
- Externalities and risks are real and measurable at sector scale (energy) and case-by-case (incidents). See IEA and the AI Incident Database.
- Governance is tightening: EU AI Act is the most comprehensive regime to date; U.S. EO 14110 and NIST RMF set process-focused guardrails; ISO/IEC 42001 provides an auditable management framework.

9) Suggested directions to explore further (with data-friendly angles)
- Benchmarking and evaluation: Compare model performance over time on MMLU, Big-Bench, and real-world exams (GPT-4 TR and AI Index 2024 have tables/figures you can cite).
- Energy and sustainability: Use IEA projections to estimate AI’s share of data center load in your country or region; assess mitigation via efficient hardware and low-carbon power (see Patterson et al.).
- Organizational returns and risks: Summarize RCTs/field studies (above) and discuss where results may or may not generalize; identify risk mitigations aligned to NIST RMF functions (Map–Measure–Manage–Govern).
- Policy landscape comparison: EU AI Act vs. U.S. EO/NIST vs. China’s measures—map obligations for high-risk uses, transparency, incident reporting, and foundation model requirements.

