 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-11-27 07:32:23,195 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise, data-backed snapshot to help you zero in on the best-value options and what to expect next month, using Brazil’s official FIPE reference (the same source dealers, insurers, and banks quote). All prices below come directly from the FIPE API and show “Reference: November 2025.” Where applicable, I include the FIPE code for verification.

How I built this
- Source: FIPE API (parallelum.com.br/fipe/api). We fetched brand/model price lists and used the latest reference month available (Nov/2025).
- Coverage: Cars from major brands (Fiat, Chevrolet, Ford, Renault, VW, Toyota, Honda, Peugeot, Audi, BMW, Volvo, Jeep, Kia, Mercedes-Benz), plus some light trucks/vans (Shineray, Iveco).
- Note on “Year 32000”: In FIPE, 32000 denotes zero‑km (new) pricing.
- Timestamp: 2025-11-27 UTC.

Key takeaways at a glance
- If you want the absolute lowest purchase price, older compact hatches/sedans from the 1990s frequently list under R$ 10,000 in FIPE (examples below with codes).
- Light-duty microtrucks (e.g., Shineray 2015) sit around R$ 27–32k—among the most affordable utility options in the database today.
- New (zero‑km) entries we could pull skewed to premium and commercial segments (R$ 300k+). Mainstream new-car entries were partially blocked by API SSL errors for some brands (Hyundai, BYD, Chery, etc.), so I’m not showing incomplete or unverified numbers there.
- Next month: FIPE is updated monthly. Use the same model FIPE codes below to re-check December’s table; movements month-over-month are typically incremental and reflect depreciation/new-month adjustments. For purchase timing, year-end promos exist at the dealership level, but FIPE will only reflect list/reference movements, not temporary dealer incentives.

